          "text": "**Observations**\n\n- A large number of W1 rows are the untouched initialisation pattern `0.020 0.030 0.050 … 0.530` (the first 16 primes /100). Those are pixels whose weights never received gradient, i.e. pixels that are exactly zero in every training image. Their positions: all of row 0 except columns 12–15; columns 0–3 and 24–27 of row 1; columns 0–1 and 26–27 of row 2; column 0–1 / 27 of rows 3–5; a single column‑0 or column‑27 pixel in a few rows; and symmetric gaps in the bottom corners (rows 25–27, cols 0–3 and 24–27). Everything else – including column 0 and column 27 from about row 6 down, and the bottom row from column 4 to 23 – is trained. So the dead region is only the four corners plus most of the very top row; the content fills the frame edge‑to‑edge and top‑to‑bottom, with the centre of the top row and most of the bottom row occupied.\n- The trained weights are smooth, large, blob‑like regions rather than thin oriented stroke detectors. E.g. unit 7 is strongly negative (≈‑0.4…‑0.55) across the whole upper‑central area (rows 3–12) and much of the middle, i.e. it fires when the top/centre of the frame is empty (a low, wide object). Unit 14 is similar (negative top centre, positive bottom‑left). Unit 2 is negative in the upper centre and positive in a horizontal band around rows 7–13 / 18–23 at the sides. Unit 3 and unit 0 have positive blocks on the right/bottom‑right (rows 17–22, cols 20–27 for unit 0, ≈0.4–0.6) and negative on the left – lateral/asymmetric silhouettes. Unit 5 and 12 are positive in a broad lower‑left patch (rows 20–26, cols 2–12), units 13/14 strongly negative there. Unit 11 is positive in the upper middle column band, unit 9 positive in rows ~18–24 at columns 8–10. These look like templates for filled silhouettes of different heights/widths and left/right asymmetry, not for digit strokes.\n- W2 is dense with mixed ±0.5–1.1 weights; every hidden unit contributes to many classes, typical of silhouette‑shape features that have to be combined to separate visually similar classes. Output biases are modest; class 5 has the largest positive bias (0.37), class 8 the most negative (‑0.43).\n\n**Reasoning**\n\n- MNIST / EMNIST / KMNIST are centred in a 20×20 box with a ~4‑pixel blank margin: a trained net on those would show the untouched‑initialisation pattern on entire rows 0–3 and 24–27 and columns 0–3 and 24–27. Here only the corners and the top row's edges are dead, while column 0, column 27 and the bottom row are live. That rules out the digit/character sets.\n- Natural‑image datasets (e.g. grayscale CIFAR) would have no permanently‑zero pixels at all, so the dead corners rule them out too.\n- The dead pattern – corners empty, centre of top row and most of bottom row occupied, both side columns occupied in the middle rows – is exactly what the Fashion‑MNIST preprocessing produces: each item is trimmed and its longest side scaled to 28 px, then centred, so tall garments touch rows 0/27 and wide items (bags, shoes) touch columns 0/27, while corners stay black.\n- The hidden units being broad silhouette/mask templates (tall‑vs‑short, left‑heavy footwear shapes, upper‑body shoulder regions) rather than stroke detectors further fits clothing silhouettes. The dense, mixed W2 fits a problem where several classes (shirt/T‑shirt/pullover/coat; sandal/sneaker/boot) are confusable and need combinations of features.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion‑MNIST (Zalando article images, 28×28 grayscale, 60k train / 10k test).\n\n**Classes:** 0 T‑shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 85%",
